1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,435
Here's how to reverse engineer CoSchedule from a written spec.

2
00:00:03,435 --> 00:00:07,140
Claude passed all twenty-seven checks, and found eight gaps in my spec.

3
00:00:07,950 --> 00:00:09,900
We gave Claude one Markdown file.

4
00:00:09,913 --> 00:00:13,313
It copies the part of CoSchedule a small marketing team actually uses.

5
00:00:13,454 --> 00:00:16,022
One calendar for every channel, colored by channel.

6
00:00:16,057 --> 00:00:19,718
A record per piece, with an owner, a status and a publish date.

7
00:00:19,736 --> 00:00:22,134
An editor's approval before anything is scheduled.

8
00:00:22,126 --> 00:00:24,290
And campaigns you can move as a block.

9
00:00:25,183 --> 00:00:26,983
The core rule is approval.

10
00:00:26,975 --> 00:00:28,967
Every piece moves through five steps.

11
00:00:28,980 --> 00:00:32,679
Idea, writing, in review, scheduled, published.

12
00:00:32,692 --> 00:00:35,217
The only way into scheduled is an editor's approval.

13
00:00:35,209 --> 00:00:37,135
And nobody approves their own piece.

14
00:00:37,167 --> 00:00:41,628
When Maya, an editor, sends her own post for review, it goes to Dana instead.

15
00:00:42,517 --> 00:00:45,255
Claude built it in four phases, one prompt each.

16
00:00:45,290 --> 00:00:48,391
The foundation, with channels, campaigns and settings.

17
00:00:48,447 --> 00:00:51,463
Pieces, the calendar, the backlog and the pipeline.

18
00:00:51,434 --> 00:00:52,703
Review and publishing.

19
00:00:52,718 --> 00:00:57,035
Then reminders, overdue flags, the weekly digest and campaign moves.

20
00:00:57,070 --> 00:01:02,043
After every phase, the acceptance checks ran in a real browser, from fresh seed data.

21
00:01:02,950 --> 00:01:04,089
Here's what it built.

22
00:01:04,230 --> 00:01:09,187
The month calendar puts each piece on its date in its channel color, and flags the overdue video.

23
00:01:09,350 --> 00:01:11,469
The pipeline board counts every status.

24
00:01:11,482 --> 00:01:15,607
A piece in review shows Approve and Request changes, with the full activity log.

25
00:01:15,749 --> 00:01:18,786
The weekly digest lists what publishes in the next seven days.

26
00:01:18,906 --> 00:01:21,144
And on a phone, the month becomes an agenda.

27
00:01:22,050 --> 00:01:27,337
In the final browser run, all twenty-seven checks passed, along with sixty-three automated tests.

28
00:01:28,217 --> 00:01:30,401
But the more useful result is what broke.

29
00:01:30,479 --> 00:01:32,535
The build exposed eight gaps in the spec.

30
00:01:32,484 --> 00:01:34,049
Places where Claude had to guess.

31
00:01:34,105 --> 00:01:38,269
Sign-in told strangers to ask an editor to add them, but no screen managed people.

32
00:01:38,278 --> 00:01:42,702
One part of the spec let editors withdraw any piece, while another said owner only.

33
00:01:42,843 --> 00:01:47,438
And a daily job at eight in the morning, team time, can't be one UTC cron schedule.

34
00:01:47,536 --> 00:01:49,473
Every gap is now fixed in the spec.

35
00:01:50,350 --> 00:01:52,854
Passing every check isn't the same as production-ready.

36
00:01:52,867 --> 00:01:58,635
We didn't test production sign-in, sending email, the scheduled daily job, or several servers writing at once.

37
00:01:58,670 --> 00:02:01,004
Those are yours to check before a team relies on it.

38
00:02:01,883 --> 00:02:03,341
So is it worth building?

39
00:02:03,504 --> 00:02:07,949
CoSchedule Starter is twenty-nine dollars a month, billed annually, for one user.

40
00:02:08,069 --> 00:02:11,256
Professional is forty-nine, with two users included.

41
00:02:11,269 --> 00:02:14,534
Campaigns are only on the Suite plan, from sixty-nine dollars a month.

42
00:02:14,540 --> 00:02:18,879
If you want posts published straight to social networks, with analytics, pay for CoSchedule.

43
00:02:18,935 --> 00:02:23,866
If your team just needs the plan, you mostly need the calendar, the approval step, and the reminders.

44
00:02:24,750 --> 00:02:26,294
Everything is in one file.

45
00:02:26,329 --> 00:02:33,183
The data model, seventeen rules, seed data, four phase prompts, and all twenty-seven checks, with the eight fixes already in.

46
00:02:33,933 --> 00:02:37,326
The full spec is free at personal software factory dot com.

47
00:02:37,326 --> 00:02:39,396
If you build it, tell me what broke.

48
00:02:39,396 --> 00:02:39,856
I'm Sam.

49
00:02:39,856 --> 00:02:40,833
See you tomorrow.

